\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The geometric fact the paper rests on. \textbf  {(a)} CGL nodes cluster quadratically at the boundary; a uniform grid does not. \textbf  {(b)} The Clenshaw--Curtis weights therefore vary by $78\times $ across the domain at $N=24$, so $W\neq  cI$ and consequently $K^{*}\neq  K^{\top }$. On a uniform grid the two coincide, which is why a Fourier-parameterised operator never encounters the distinction. \textbf  {(c)} The Chebyshev basis assumes no periodicity at the domain ends.}}{5}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:weights}{{1}{5}{The geometric fact the paper rests on. \textbf {(a)} CGL nodes cluster quadratically at the boundary; a uniform grid does not. \textbf {(b)} The Clenshaw--Curtis weights therefore vary by $78\times $ across the domain at $N=24$, so $W\neq cI$ and consequently $K^{*}\neq K^{\top }$. On a uniform grid the two coincide, which is why a Fourier-parameterised operator never encounters the distinction. \textbf {(c)} The Chebyshev basis assumes no periodicity at the domain ends}{figure.caption.6}{}}
